---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Deep Research - AI Training Guide">
	<PageShell chapterId="competitive-research" subChapterId="deep-research">
		<div class="prose space-y-6">
			<p>
				The previous section walked through competitive research as a manual, round-by-round conversation. That is still a good way to work when you want to steer each step. But most AI tools now offer a mode that automates the whole loop: you ask one well-framed question, and the tool plans its own searches, reads many sources, and comes back with a written, cited report. In the Claude app this is called{" "}
				<strong>Research</strong>, and it is what we expect people at KG to reach for first.
			</p>

			<h3>
				What Deep Research Does
			</h3>

			<p>
				With Research turned on, Claude acts as an agent rather than a chat partner. It breaks your question into sub-questions, runs multiple web searches in parallel, follows leads across sources, and works through the open questions systematically before writing up an answer with citations. A single run can take several minutes and read far more pages than you would open by hand. If you have connected Google Workspace, it can pull from your Gmail, Calendar, and Docs alongside the web.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="info" title="Turning it on">
				<p>
					In the Claude app, click the <strong>+</strong> button at the bottom left of the chat, then <strong>Research</strong>. A blue indicator shows it is active; click it again to turn it off. Web search needs to be enabled for Research to work. It is available on the paid plans (Pro, Max, Team, Enterprise) across web, desktop, and mobile. See Anthropic's{" "}
					<a href="https://support.claude.com/en/articles/11088861-using-research-on-claude-ai" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800">Using Research on Claude</a>{" "}
					for the current steps.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<h3>
				When to Use It (and When Not To)
			</h3>

			<p>
				Deep Research earns its time on broad, multi-part questions where the value is in breadth and synthesis. Manual prompting is still the better tool when you want to stay in control of each step or you are working from source material you have already gathered.
			</p>

			<div class="space-y-4 my-8">
				<Callout variant="success" title="Good fit for Research">
					<ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
						<li>"Map the competitive landscape for agency-focused project management tools, with positioning and pricing for each."</li>
						<li>Questions that span many sources you would otherwise open one at a time.</li>
						<li>A first-pass survey of a market or category you are new to.</li>
					</ul>
				</Callout>

				<Callout variant="warning" title="Reach for manual prompting instead">
					<ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
						<li>You want to steer each step and react to what comes back before going further.</li>
						<li>You are analyzing specific pages or documents you have already collected.</li>
						<li>You need a fast, narrow answer, not a multi-minute report.</li>
					</ul>
				</Callout>
			</div>

			<p>
				The framing habits from Setting Up Your Research matter more here, not less. Deep Research runs with the question you give it and does not stop to ask clarifying questions partway through, so the specific-questions and context-about-you work up front is what determines whether the report is useful. A vague prompt produces a long, generic report; a focused one produces something you can act on.
			</p>

			<h3>
				Verify the Output, Every Time
			</h3>

			<p>
				A cited report is easy to trust and easy to over-trust. The citations are the starting point for verification, not proof the work is done. Deep Research reads more sources than you can quickly check, which makes it more important, not less, to spot-check the claims and figures you plan to use before anything lands in a deliverable. The next section, Citing Sources, covers how to do that. The short version: if you did not click the link and confirm it yourself, do not ship it.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="tip" title="Practical tip">
				<p>
					Have Research write its report to a file, the same way you would with manual prompting. A cited markdown report in your research folder is a durable artifact you can verify, refine, and feed into your final deliverable, rather than a result buried in a chat thread.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<h3>Further Reading</h3>
			<ul class="text-sm space-y-2 my-4">
				<li>
					<a href="https://support.claude.com/en/articles/11088861-using-research-on-claude-ai" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Using Research on Claude</a> — Anthropic's help article on enabling and using the Research feature
				</li>
				<li>
					<a href="https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800" target="_blank" rel="noopener">How we built our multi-agent research system</a> — Anthropic, on how Research plans and runs parallel searches under the hood
				</li>
			</ul>
		</div>
	</PageShell>
</MainLayout>
